"# aos-gdb and aos.py: drive a running agent from Python, stop it when a condition holds, look, go on\n\n`aos.py` is the Python face of aos for people (an agent does not need it: you use tools). `aos-gdb NAME` opens Python with `agent`, `daemon_ctl`, `std` and `defs` ready (`from aos.py import agent, daemon_ctl, std`; `from aos.py.agent import defs`), and `a = agent.attach(NAME)` (`--daemon D`, or `$AOS_DAEMON`). Without NAME only the modules are ready. Every call prints the aos command behind it on stderr (`$ ...`).\n\nOne rule: `aos-agent --daemon D --target N X Y --flag v ARG` is `agent.attach(N, daemon=D).X.Y(ARG, flag=v)`; one call is exactly one command. `a.say(\"hi\")` returns the letter name; `json=True` adds `--json` and returns parsed JSON (JSON lines become a list, empty is None); otherwise you get stdout as it is (empty is None). `True` is a bare flag, `False`/`None` leave it out, a list repeats the flag, a trailing `_` is dropped (`from_=`). `ctl add / rm / ls / set-def / who` take no `--target`: call them on the module (`agent.ctl.ls()`). `daemon_ctl.X(...)` is `aos-daemon-ctl X ...`. The only error is `Failed`: the command's own error line, with `e.code`, `e.argv`, `e.exit`.\n\nNames under `a` are `aos-agent` subcommands, plus two extensions that live only in the library: `a.bp` (breakpoints) and `a.look` (read the stack's files: `where()`, `history(n)`, `observed(n)`, `request()`, `queue()`, `stderr(n)`, `file(rel)`). Any other name is an AttributeError.\n\nBreakpoints are built around the breakpoint: add, rm, set, ls, get. `a.bp.add.<kind>(...)` returns the breakpoint as an object: `b.id` is its number (an int from 1; a removed number is never used again, also after reopening the REPL: the counter is in the agent's stack, `gdb/next_id.json`), `b.kind` its kind (before_tool, tool_error, watch, ...), and every other field of `a.bp.get(b)` (`b.enabled`, `b.fired`, `b.here`, ...) is read again each time you read it; after `a.bp.rm(b)` reading a field fails (`BreakpointGone`). `rm`, `set` and `get` take the object, the number (`3` or `\"3\"`) or a name you gave with `id=\"mine\"` (a name may not be all digits). Add one with `a.bp.add.<kind>(...)`; the kinds are the hook events (before_tool, after_tool, before_queue, after_queue, brain_failed, tool_added, after_start, after_stop), `tool_error` and `watch`. Every kind has the same shape: an optional first argument (the tool name; for brain_failed the error code; kinds without a name take none), then keywords `sh=`, `py=`, `times=`, `once=`, `id=`, `replace=` (watch also `every_ms=`, `also=`). Typing `a.bp.add` lists them.\n\nTwo kinds of breakpoint, one row of numbers. A trigger is an ordinary hook on that agent (name `gdb_<id>` with the number zero-padded to 4 digits, e.g. `gdb_0003`, so names list and hooks run in number order; `b.id` is still `3`; handler `lib/tools/agent/gdb_trigger`). A watcher (`a.bp.add.watch(...)`) is a separate proc on the daemon table (name `gdbw_<id>`, e.g. `gdbw_0004`) that looks at the agent's files and pauses it; its number is taken from the counters of every agent it watches, so it never clashes with their triggers.\n\nHow it stops depends on the event, not on an option:\n\n- `before_tool`, `before_queue` (not happened yet): the trigger **holds** the agent inside its hook. The tool has not run; the table still says running, on purpose: aos-agent and the daemon do not know about gdb (kept decoupled), so use `a.bp.why()` / `a.bp.held()` to see a breakpoint stop. `a.bp.resume()` lets that call run as it was; `aos-agent ctl resume` from a shell also lets it run. There is no way to let it go without running the tool.\n- other events (`after_tool`, `brain_failed`, ...) and watchers: `aos-agent ctl pause`; the loop stops after the current step (after_tool: the result is in history, the brain has not seen it). `a.bp.resume()` sends `ctl resume`.\n\nThree controls, like a debugger's; each waits until the agent stops again, then shows where (the first line reads like `stopped at breakpoint 1 (before_tool: write)`). Their one argument is `wait=` (default True). `wait=False` sends the action and returns at once with an object: `p.is_done()` says whether it is done (stopped, or sure it will not stop), `p.wait()` gives the same result the waiting call gives (errors such as `Timeout` come out there), `p.cancel()` only stops waiting: nothing that was sent is taken back (a step's pause still stops it when the step ends), and then `p.wait().outcome` is `cancelled`:\n\n- `a.bp.resume()`: go on. Held: let the call run. Paused: `ctl resume`. Not stopped (just after `a.say`): sends nothing, only waits.\n- `a.bp.step()`: go one step of the fsm (one of idle, think, wait, act; while the brain is thinking, wait runs again and again) and stop. Held before a tool: the tool runs, and it stops when that act step ends.\n- `a.bp.pause()`: stop now, without a breakpoint (`ctl pause`); already stopped = sends nothing.\n\n```python\nb = a.bp.add.before_tool(\"write\")                    # held before write runs; b.id == 1, b.kind == \"before_tool\"\na.bp.add.after_tool(\"write\"); a.bp.add.tool_error(times=2)\na.bp.add.after_tool(sh=\"grep -q secret || exit 10\")  # event JSON on stdin; exit 0 = hit, 10 = no hit\na.bp.add.before_tool(py=lambda ev: ev[\"arguments\"].get(\"path\", \"\").endswith(\".env\"), once=True)\na.bp.add.watch(py=lambda t: len(t.history(0)) > 20, every_ms=500)   # t = the target's files; t.mem = a dict kept between looks\na.bp.add.watch(also=[\"bob\"], sh=\"test -f flag || exit 10\", times=3)  # one watcher, two agents; cwd = each target's stack\na.say(\"hi\"); a.bp.resume()                          # waits; \"stopped at breakpoint 1 (before_tool: write)\"\na.bp.why(); a.bp.held(); a.look.where(); a.look.history(3)\na.bp.step()                                          # write runs; stops when this step ends\na.bp.resume()                                        # go on, wait for the next stop\np = a.bp.resume(wait=False); p.is_done(); p.wait()   # do not wait; ask later; same result\nb.fired                                              # read again every time\na.bp.ls(); a.bp.get(b); a.bp.set(b, enabled=False, times=3, once=True); a.bp.rm(b); a.bp.clear()\na.bp.pause()                                          # stop it now without a breakpoint\n```\n\n- `a.bp.set(b, ...)` returns the breakpoint too (the same kind of object `add` returns; fields are read again every time).\n- `a.bp.get(b)` / `a.bp.ls()` rows (numbers first, then names): id, kind (the kind it was added as; watch for watchers), how (hold or pause), about, enabled, times, once, seen, fired, spent, last_error, and `here` (it is stopped at this one now). `set` changes enabled, times and once only; to change the condition or the event, add again with `id=b, replace=True` (a number you did not get from add cannot be chosen).\n- While held: letters still queue; a `ctl pause` or `ctl stop` sent meanwhile applies after the tool ran (`why()` says so). `ctl sleep`, `rm` or a daemon shutdown cut the hold: the tool did not run, and the same call is held again when the agent comes back. Removing or disabling a breakpoint does not end a hold already on it.\n- Watchers are one step late (they poll). `times` = rounds in a row; after a stop the condition must be seen false once before it can stop again; paused or held targets are not checked. A broken watcher condition is `last_error`; a dead watcher does not touch its targets.\n- A trigger is a plain hook whose handler prints nothing and, when its condition or files are broken, prints one warning line and exits 0: the agent does not stop or restart. `why()` lists notes since the last resume (`by`: hook, watch, repl, ctl), whether they belong to this stop, and `.breakpoint` = the id it is stopped at (`== b` works).\n- When it never stops: idle (stage idle, nothing in its queue, no pause on the way) for `a.bp.idle_s` seconds (default 1) = the call returns \"did not stop: it is idle\"; its loop process gone = \"did not stop: it is not running\". While waiting, one line on stderr every `a.bp.report_s` seconds (default 10, 0 = never). `a.bp.timeout_s` (default None = no limit) makes it raise `Timeout` (a `Failed` and a `TimeoutError`); these are settings on `a.bp`, not arguments (the only argument is `wait=`; with wait=False the stderr line is not printed). The result has `.outcome`: stopped, idle, gone or interrupted.\n- Ctrl-C while waiting only stops the waiting: the agent goes on, nothing is left behind (a pause a step sent and that is not applied yet is taken back). After `a.bp.pause()` the pause stays sent.\n- Python conditions run in another process: they see `json`, `re`, `os` and builtins; pass other values as defaults (`lambda ev, x=x: ...`). They are refused at once when they use outer variables or unknown names.\n- Do not put breakpoints on another agent unless you were asked to debug it: it stops that agent (aos/rules.json). More: `aos-gdb --help`, `help(a.bp)` and `a.bp.add` in the REPL, proto8/py/aos/apps/gdb/README.md.\n"
